what's going on everyone i'm richard  this is richard on data  i just hit 5 000 subscribers and i have  each and every one of you all to thank  for that  so keep watching this video and i'm  going to talk about why i started this  channel in the first place  and then where it's going in the year  2021  [Music]  so i started this channel in november of  last year  meaning i've been doing this for just  over a year at this point  now that i've hit 5000 subscribers to  tell you the truth  i couldn't think of anything too special  for this occasion  but i do think that it presents a good  opportunity both for the new people and  for the people who've been around for a  longer time  i'm going to talk about what motivated  me to start a youtube channel in the  first place  and then i have big plans for the year  2021 so i do want to talk about those as  well  before i do any of that hit the like  button for the youtube algorithm  subscribe to my channel if you haven't  already hit the notification bell so  that youtube notifies you whenever i  upload a video  and then lastly if you guys would be  willing to support me over on patreon  i'll have a link to that in the  description of the video it's massively  appreciated  alright so why did i start the channel  well  prior to starting it i had had the  desire to embark on some kind of  entrepreneurial endeavor for quite some  time  there's a couple reasons for that first  and foremost is i think  us humans do have it embedded in our dna  to want to create things  it's way too easy to get sucked into the  monotony of day after day  consuming social media consuming one  episode after another of netflix  and i really do think these sorts of  behaviors are causing my generation the  millennials  to be a little bit more depressed and  anxious than previous generations were  if we haven't put our mark on the  universe in some capacity  there's just this sort of void inside of  us it's a fact that many of us use  youtube  every single day to learn how to do new  things  and if nobody out there took the time to  actually create that content  we would all be a lot worse off for that  so that's the first thing the second  reason is  youtube is basically the definition of a  passive income model  so i got to be honest with all of you  guys i really  don't like working in a 9-5 job  yes you get paid for the 40 hours of  time that you put in  and particularly in the data science  field that pay tends to be extremely  generous but what you give up for that  that is  your time is of insane magnitude  i think there's a lot of people who get  the idea of job security into their  heads  but as i think a lot of people figured  out this year during the pandemic  that is a complete and utter myth you'll  always be able to build an income  so long as you have skills that are  desired in the broader marketplace  but there are absolutely no guarantees  in the world with your job  something happens to it you get fired  you want to move  who the hell cares your job is not going  to last forever  to be fair that's true of youtube and  the income streams that it brings me as  well  but here's the key difference it's  scalable for a while  i've made it my goal to produce one  programming or stats tutorial per week  as well as one other type of video i  haven't always lived up to that  but that's at least been my objective  here's the thing  six months ago almost from today  actually  i hit 1 000 subscribers on this channel  now all of a sudden i'm reaching five  times as many people as i used to  roughly but to be honest i don't really  actually have to do  that much more work than when i was only  reaching 1 000 subscribers  and that to me is an absolutely  beautiful thing the key here is scale  yeah i guess theoretically i could work  two 40-hour per week jobs  then i'm doubling the impact that i have  on the world and  roughly i'm doubling the amount of money  that i have coming in  but who in their right mind wants to do  that i work to live  i do not live to work but what i do here  can be built upon  theoretically at some point in the  future i could have 500  000 subscribers on this channel so i'm  reaching 100 times as many people as i  am today  and i have roughly around 100 times the  amount of income coming in  all that with not that much extra work  and let's just say that if i 100  x to the current amount of income that i  have coming in from youtube  the current nine-to-five job that i work  would be optional at best  so yeah the passive income model  associated with youtube  is a wonderful thing and that's  absolutely key then you might think  okay i'm a data scientist but why make a  youtube channel all about that  well i'm going to approach that question  both from a personal perspective  and a market-based perspective and i'm  going to tie the latter  back to this broader conversation about  income first of all back when i was in  graduate school  i used to run lab sessions for a really  large intro stats class  so i would do a lecture for about 20  minutes then we would work on stats  problems together  come back at the end and go over the  answers that kind of thing  and i absolutely loved it i loved  getting the opportunity to inspire  people  maybe even to major in the field and of  about  30 plus instructors who were involved in  this program  i would consistently get either the  highest or close to the highest  student reviews of all of them so  to me it was a really amazing thing and  obviously it kind of got me thinking  now i have no interest whatsoever in  becoming either a professor  or like a high school teacher and also  i found that when i was teaching the  same class over and over again  that the magic of it kind of started to  wear off but i do  love education there's no question about  that it's just not something that i  currently do  or frankly want to do within the  confines of a nine to five job  so again this is why i love the youtube  model so much  it allows me to create content on  whatever it is that i want  and continue to progressively reach a  larger and larger audience  certainly a lot larger than i can reach  from within a classroom  i think the first question that anybody  has to ask if they're thinking about  making a youtube channel however  is do you actually have something to say  that is there's  tons of different channels out there on  this platform  now are you actually putting something  out there that's different from what's  already there  and my answer to that was and remains a  resounding yes  for example i think there's a  disproportionate amount of content out  there that's all about focusing on ml  now i say it again and again in my  videos i love  ml and i do videos on it but it's only a  small piece of the overall puzzle that  is data science  i think what a lot of people learning  data science really want  is some kind of structure to the madness  i make jokes about it all the time but  there's  way too much out there that a lot of  people feel expected to know  and i do completely reject outright the  notion that  every single job out there is different  no  there are some universal principles that  we can think about  that are going to apply to the vast  majority of people  more on that later but then i think  there's way too many people out there  who are proclaiming to be gurus  and to be quite honest there's almost no  such thing as a true data science guru  because the field is just too new and it  evolves too quickly  from day one when i launched this  channel i would try to be completely  straightforward with what i  am that's somebody with five that's now  six years of experience doing data  science  who happens to know some things and  when you work on actual jobs that's the  type of person that you're probably  going to learn the most from  for the most part it's not going to be  from some professorial or some  self-proclaimed guru type but i try to  be as straightforward as possible  and to conduct this channel with  integrity and associated with that  is the fact that i never have any plans  on this channel to create any sort of  course  there's so much information out there on  the web on data science  that most people are not going to have  to spend a penny in order to learn the  particular topic that they want to learn  granted there is value in courses in  terms of how they organize and structure  different content  but honestly that is so close to what i  already want to do with this channel  which is to provide some sort of  structure to people  that i really at least at this time see  absolutely no purpose in starting up a  course on this channel  and honestly i would feel like a total  sleazeball for doing it  to be totally fair of course i wouldn't  be doing this channel  just as charity work if it wasn't  bringing me in some kind of revenue  and yes i do collect revenue from this  channel through a combination of  ads patrons and amazon affiliate links  and yeah i know there's a lot of you out  there who don't like seeing the ads  but the way i see that that's a pretty  acceptable trade-off for having freely  available information that i'm not  asking you to pay for  so that's a perfect segue into what's  the plan for this channel in 2021  well to put it simply i do love the idea  of this channel being a one-stop shop  for you to learn data science  i've put together a study pathway for  prospective data scientists where i  stress that  first and foremost you should learn  statistics i make the case that  every data professional out there pretty  much should learn sql  and then data scientists should pick one  of r or python  and then master it well over the course  of 2021  i'm going to build out a sql tutorial  series i'm going to finish my  series on statistics and art tutorials  and i'm going to continue to bring you  my perspectives on the latest and  greatest news and trends  that are happening in the data science  world something that i will say is that  i've received an increased number  of requests to not forget about data and  business analysts  to broaden my focus beyond just data  science  so to you guys out there i'm going to  try to start making some content  directed towards you as well  now one thing that i do with this  channel on occasion as well  is to talk about real life topics like  the election  the virus and the stock market and try  to bring some degree of data driven  perspective to those things  i do that because on one hand those  topics are extremely fun to talk about  but i do also think it's incredibly  educational to learn how to think of  these things  in the same way that you would a real  world data problem  again there's way too many videos out  there about how to create some magical  algorithm that's not actually magical to  predict something  meanwhile there's not enough videos out  there about  the underlying data how to question that  the assumptions behind whatever it is  you're doing  and at the end of the day in virtually  all real world business data problems  those sorts of things are where the  majority of your impact is going to come  from  so to be honest i don't have plans to  change around all that much with this  channel  except that i'm going to try to be as  consistent as possible  i'm going to complete out tutorial  series on sql  r and julia as well as continue to talk  about  general data related topics and the data  science industry  and hopefully when it's all said and  done this can be  one central location where you can go  and learn all the different facets of  data science  with that said i do make a concerted  effort to read the comments on my videos  and some of the best video ideas that  i've ever had  come from you guys so by all means  whenever you guys have ideas of things  you'd like me to cover  let me know and i will try to serve you  guys as best as i possibly can  the final thing i'll say in this video  is thank you to all 5  000 plus of you who've subscribed and  those of you who tune in  you've made this all both possible and  worthwhile  and frankly i'm just getting started the  goal is  50 000 by the end of next year thanks  again  and here's to 2021 and the new chapters  that it's going to bring in all of our  lives and in our data science journeys  so until next time richard on data

